{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e22f4082-815d-4291-8ec0-471a24337109","agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Build the gate execution framework for the M8.5-C2 qualification protocol. Create the file `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/gates.py`.\n\nThis file implements gates 1, 2, 3 (partially), and 10 — the gates that don't require Newton continuation or time integration.\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the M8.5-C2 Build Unit commission. The output ledger is `ledger/OUTPUT_LEDGER.jsonl`, an append-only JSONL file. The first GATE record written makes the attempt LIVE.\n\n## Required modules already built:\n- `build/group.py` — 120 icosians, character table, all 9 unitary representations, `multiplicity()`, `DIMS`, `IRREP_NAMES`, `pi_n_unitary()`, `quat_to_su2()`, `ALL_REPS`\n- `build/sections.py` — `build_basis_object()`, `total_modes()`, `compute_intertwiners()`, `basis_hash()`\n- `build/operators.py` — `laplacian_eigenvalues()`, `linear_operator()`, `real_jacobian()`, `K_defect()`, `J_defect()`\n- `build/quadrature.py` — `hopf_rule()`, `production_rule()`, `monitor_rule()`, `synthesis()`, `analysis()`, `eval_pi_n_unitary_at_nodes()`\n- `build/arena.py` — `build_arena_registry()`, `AGREEMENT_RUNGS`, `CONTROL_B_RUNGS`, `ALL_RUNGS`, `NONTRIVIAL_SECTORS`\n- `build/ledger_util.py` — `init_clock()`, `append_record()`, `gate_record()`, `resource_record()`, `arm_allocation_record()`, `disposition_record()`, `coverage_record()`\n\n## Gate 1: G-LIN wiring\n\nFor EVERY sector (R0-R8) at EVERY rung in ALL_RUNGS = [24, 32, 36, 40, 44, 48, 52, 60]:\n1. Assemble the linear diagonal operator A = diag(-n(n+2)) using `linear_operator(rho, N)`\n2. Compute K(A) = ||A - A^H||₂ and J(A) = max|Im λ(A)| using `K_defect(A)` and `J_defect(A)`\n3. Check K ≤ 100 * eps_mach * ||A||₂ and J ≤ 100 * eps_mach * ||A||₂\n   where eps_mach = 2.22e-16 (binary64 machine epsilon)\n4. MUTATION ARM: inject anti-Hermitian coupling between two retained modes. Specifically, create a copy of A and add an anti-Hermitian perturbation (e.g., set A[0,1] += 1.0, A[1,0] -= 1.0 for a 2×2 block). This must make K or J go red (exceed the threshold).\n5. Write a GATE record for each (sector, rung) combination.\n\nThe arena_id for sector rho at rung N is:\n- 'A-R0-N{N}' for R0\n- 'A-SECTOR-{rho}-N{N}' for nontrivial sectors\n\n## Gate 2: G-LABEL\n\nFor every retained basis element at every level n ≤ 3N (where N is the rung):\n1. The Laplacian eigenvalue for level n is λ_n = n(n+2)\n2. Verify through the sampling map: synthesize the basis element at random S³ points, apply the Laplacian (multiply coefficients by -n(n+2)), and verify the eigenvalue is correct\n3. Actually, the Casimir check is simpler: verify that each basis function's Laplacian eigenvalue matches n(n+2). Since the basis is organized by level, this is just checking that the level labels are correct.\n4. More precisely: the \"sampling map\" means: take a basis element e_α at level n. Synthesize it at quadrature nodes. Apply the Laplacian in the frequency domain (multiply coefficient α by -n(n+2)). Synthesize the result. The ratio of synthesized Laplacian to synthesized original should be -n(n+2) at every node.\n5. MUTATION: mislabel one element (use a basis element from level n but claim its eigenvalue is (n+1)(n+3)). Must go red.\n6. Arena: the certified § 3 basis elements themselves.\n7. Run over n ≤ 3N at the agreement ladder rungs.\n\nActually, a more practical interpretation: The eigenvalue of the Laplacian for level-n basis functions is n(n+2). Gate 2 verifies that the Laplacian through the sampling map (synthesis → pointwise action → analysis) returns the correct eigenvalue. This requires:\n- For each basis function at level n, form its coefficient vector (1 in position α, 0 elsewhere)\n- Apply the Laplacian: multiply by -n(n+2)\n- Synthesize to nodes, then analyze back\n- The round-trip should return the original coefficient times -n(n+2)\n\nSo the check is really a round-trip test of the synthesis/analysis at each level, verifying that the eigenvalue label is correct.\n\nSince the basis is already organized by level, and the Laplacian is diagonal with entry -n(n+2) for each mode at level n, the gate checks that:\n- Each mode is correctly assigned its level\n- The synthesis/analysis round-trip preserves the level structure\n\nRun at agreement ladder rungs, over all sectors, up to level 3N.\n\n## Gate 10: Executable partition\n\nThe coverage check has two sides:\n1. Every arena in the registry was exercised by its owning gates (tracked by noting which arenas were used during gates 1-9)\n2. The run log contains no nonlinear evaluation tagged with a nontrivial sector arena\n\nMUTATION ARMS:\n- Inject a deliberately out-of-registry nonlinear call → must be caught\n- Deliberately skip a registry arena → must trip the coverage check\n\nImplementation: maintain a set of exercised arenas. After all gates complete, check:\n- Every registry arena appears in the exercised set → pass\n- No nonlinear evaluation on a nonlinear_permitted=False arena → pass\n\nFor now, implement the coverage tracking and the check. Gates 1-9 will register their arena usage.\n\n## Structure\n\n```python\ndef run_gate_1(sectors, rungs):\n    \"\"\"Execute Gate 1: G-LIN wiring check.\"\"\"\n    \ndef run_gate_2(sectors, rungs):\n    \"\"\"Execute Gate 2: G-LABEL Casimir check.\"\"\"\n    \ndef run_gate_10(exercised_arenas, nonlinear_arenas):\n    \"\"\"Execute Gate 10: executable partition coverage.\"\"\"\n```\n\nEach function should:\n1. Time itself with `time.time()`\n2. Run the parent check\n3. Run the mutation arm(s)\n4. Write GATE record(s) via `gate_record()`\n5. Return True/False for pass/fail\n\nRead the existing modules to understand their APIs:\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/operators.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/sections.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/group.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/arena.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/ledger_util.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/quadrature.py`\n\nWrite the file, then test gate 1 at a single small rung (N=24, R0 only) to verify the logic works."},"uuid":"6d8a9bb4-1aac-4db9-9794-78e909a530fe","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:19.848Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"6d8a9bb4-1aac-4db9-9794-78e909a530fe","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_delta","addedNames":["EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","Monitor","NotebookEdit","SendMessage","TaskStop","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__complete_authentication"],"addedLines":["EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","Monitor","NotebookEdit","SendMessage","TaskStop","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__complete_authentication"],"removedNames":[],"wireHiddenNames":[],"readdedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e409c9e2-f5b4-4ea4-bc18-7d519c3ecdb6","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:19.851Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"e409c9e2-f5b4-4ea4-bc18-7d519c3ecdb6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- design: Create a design canvas - a multi-artboard visual design published as an Artifact that runs Claude Design's canvas editor (an early preview of Claude Design inside Claude Code). You DRAFT the design as .dc.html artboards laid out on one pan/zoom canvas; where saving is enabled for the user's account they refine every element visually (click-to-select, a properties panel, inline text editing, undo/redo) and Save publishes a new version for everyone, otherwise they get a view-and-export (PNG/PDF) preview of your draft. Good for UI mockups and screen flows, landing pages, marketing and social graphics, and print pieces - posters, flyers, brochures as single-page artboards; memos and reports as one flowing artboard. Use when someone wants a design, mockup, wireframe, UI or screen design, landing page, poster, flyer, brochure, banner, card, one-pager, or any visual layout they would rather tweak by hand than in code. Only for CREATING or re-seeding a canvas; an existing one is edited in its published Artifact.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever the user asks for an artifact needing any such runtime behavior.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init\n- security-review","skillCount":18,"isInitial":true,"names":["design","dataviz","artifact-design","artifact-diagramming","artifact-capabilities","update-config","keybindings-help","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","workflow-authoring","claude-in-chrome","run","init","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"32ad062f-90d1-44bb-b422-f2fd9fbd6f67","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:19.851Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"32ad062f-90d1-44bb-b422-f2fd9fbd6f67","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CeZLn9LYCM5ZyWM3htag4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll start by reading all the existing modules to understand their APIs."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4802,"cache_read_input_tokens":16388,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4802,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CeZLn8XvXci1toJRdXAUZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c6badc7c-c9a8-488c-9973-ef797313195f","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:23.043Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"c6badc7c-c9a8-488c-9973-ef797313195f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CeZLn9LYCM5ZyWM3htag4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HbdFSsBbFdYW8oKjNiJD4Z","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/operators.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4802,"cache_read_input_tokens":16388,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4802,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CeZLn8XvXci1toJRdXAUZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d6261879-21f7-42b5-99b5-68dec6899be5","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:23.668Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"d6261879-21f7-42b5-99b5-68dec6899be5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e22f4082-815d-4291-8ec0-471a24337109","agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HbdFSsBbFdYW8oKjNiJD4Z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Linear operators for the spectral Galerkin system on S^3 with binary\n2\ticosahedral (2I) symmetry.\n3\t\n4\tAssembles:\n5\t  - Laplacian eigenvalue array for any sector rho at cutoff N\n6\t  - Diagonal linear operator (Laplacian in the Galerkin basis)\n7\t  - Real Jacobian of the cubic residual R(phi; omega) = Delta phi - c1 <phi,phi> phi + omega^2 phi\n8\t  - Diagnostics: self-adjointness defect K and imaginary-spectrum defect J\n9\t\"\"\"\n10\timport numpy as np\n11\tfrom build.group import IRREP_NAMES, DIMS, multiplicity\n12\tfrom build.sections import total_modes\n13\t\n14\t\n15\tdef laplacian_eigenvalues(rho, N):\n16\t    \"\"\"Laplacian eigenvalue array for sector rho at cutoff N.\n17\t\n18\t    Returns a 1-D real array of length total_modes(rho, N).  Entry i carries\n19\t    the eigenvalue n(n+2) for the level n to which basis function i belongs.\n20\t    Modes are ordered level-major, then intertwiner-index, then multiplet-index.\n21\t    \"\"\"\n22\t    eigs = []\n23\t    for n in range(N + 1):\n24\t        m = multiplicity(rho, n)\n25\t        if m == 0:\n26\t            continue\n27\t        count = m * (n + 1)          # modes at this level\n28\t        eigs.extend([n * (n + 2)] * count)\n29\t    eigs = np.array(eigs, dtype=float)\n30\t    assert len(eigs) == total_modes(rho, N), (\n31\t        f\"eigenvalue count {len(eigs)} != total_modes({rho}, {N}) = {total_modes(rho, N)}\"\n32\t    )\n33\t    return eigs\n34\t\n35\t\n36\tdef linear_operator(rho, N):\n37\t    \"\"\"Diagonal linear operator for sector rho at cutoff N.\n38\t\n39\t    Returns a diagonal matrix of shape (m, m) where m = total_modes(rho, N),\n40\t    with diagonal entries -n(n+2) for each basis function at level n.\n41\t    This is the Galerkin representation of the Laplacian on S^3.\n42\t    \"\"\"\n43\t    eigs = laplacian_eigenvalues(rho, N)\n44\t    return np.diag(-eigs)\n45\t\n46\t\n47\tdef real_jacobian(phi, omega, rho, N, c1=1.0):\n48\t    \"\"\"Real Jacobian of the cubic residual R(phi; omega) at state (phi, omega).\n49\t\n50\t    The residual is:\n51\t        R(phi; omega) = Delta phi - c1 <phi,phi> phi + omega^2 phi\n52\t\n53\t    where <phi,phi> = phi^H phi (Hermitian inner product, real-valued) and\n54\t    Delta is diagonal with entries -n(n+2) in the Galerkin basis.\n55\t\n56\t    Because phi -> <phi,phi> phi is NOT complex-differentiable, the Jacobian\n57\t    is real-linear on R^{2m} (m = number of complex modes).  Writing\n58\t    phi = x + iy and v = [x; y] in R^{2m}:\n59\t\n60\t        J = diag([l; l]) + omega^2 I_{2m} - c1 <phi,phi> I_{2m} - 2 c1 v v^T\n61\t\n62\t    where l is the vector of Laplacian eigenvalues -n(n+2).\n63\t\n64\t    Parameters\n65\t    ----------\n66\t    phi : complex array of length m\n67\t        Current complex state vector.\n68\t    omega : float\n69\t        Frequency parameter (the Jacobian depends on omega^2).\n70\t    rho : str\n71\t        Sector name (e.g. 'R0').\n72\t    N : int\n73\t        Spectral cutoff.\n74\t    c1 : float\n75\t        Nonlinear coupling constant (default 1.0).\n76\t\n77\t    Returns\n78\t    -------\n79\t    J : ndarray of shape (2m, 2m)\n80\t        The real Jacobian matrix.\n81\t    \"\"\"\n82\t    phi = np.asarray(phi, dtype=complex).ravel()\n83\t    m = total_modes(rho, N)\n84\t    assert len(phi) == m, f\"phi has {len(phi)} entries, expected {m}\"\n85\t\n86\t    l = -laplacian_eigenvalues(rho, N)          # diagonal of Laplacian: -n(n+2)\n87\t    phi_norm_sq = np.vdot(phi, phi).real        # <phi, phi>\n88\t    v = np.concatenate([phi.real, phi.imag])     # real representation\n89\t\n90\t    # Diagonal part: (L + omega^2 I - c1 <phi,phi> I) in real coords\n91\t    diag_val = l + omega**2 - c1 * phi_norm_sq\n92\t    diag_block = np.concatenate([diag_val, diag_val])\n93\t    J = np.diag(diag_block)\n94\t\n95\t    # Rank-1 update: -2 c1 v v^T\n96\t    J -= 2.0 * c1 * np.outer(v, v)\n97\t\n98\t    return J\n99\t\n100\t\n101\tdef K_defect(A):\n102\t    \"\"\"Self-adjointness defect: ||A - A^H||_2 (spectral norm of the skew part).\n103\t\n104\t    For a real matrix, A^H = A^T, so this measures ||A - A^T||_2.\n105\t    \"\"\"\n106\t    A = np.asarray(A)\n107\t    return np.linalg.norm(A - A.conj().T, 2)\n108\t\n109\t\n110\tdef J_defect(A):\n111\t    \"\"\"Imaginary-spectrum defect: max |Im lambda(A)| over all eigenvalues.\n112\t\n113\t    A self-adjoint operator has purely real spectrum, so this measures\n114\t    departure from self-adjointness in spectral terms.\n115\t    \"\"\"\n116\t    A = np.asarray(A)\n117\t    eigvals = np.linalg.eigvals(A)\n118\t    return float(np.max(np.abs(eigvals.imag)))\n119\t"}]},"uuid":"89267438-7bdd-4b10-a91c-c1907892b4a8","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:23.674Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/operators.py","content":"\"\"\"Linear operators for the spectral Galerkin system on S^3 with binary\nicosahedral (2I) symmetry.\n\nAssembles:\n  - Laplacian eigenvalue array for any sector rho at cutoff N\n  - Diagonal linear operator (Laplacian in the Galerkin basis)\n  - Real Jacobian of the cubic residual R(phi; omega) = Delta phi - c1 <phi,phi> phi + omega^2 phi\n  - Diagnostics: self-adjointness defect K and imaginary-spectrum defect J\n\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom build.group import IRREP_NAMES, DIMS, multiplicity\nfrom build.sections import total_modes\n\n\ndef laplacian_eigenvalues(rho, N):\n    \"\"\"Laplacian eigenvalue array for sector rho at cutoff N.\n\n    Returns a 1-D real array of length total_modes(rho, N).  Entry i carries\n    the eigenvalue n(n+2) for the level n to which basis function i belongs.\n    Modes are ordered level-major, then intertwiner-index, then multiplet-index.\n    \"\"\"\n    eigs = []\n    for n in range(N + 1):\n        m = multiplicity(rho, n)\n        if m == 0:\n            continue\n        count = m * (n + 1)          # modes at this level\n        eigs.extend([n * (n + 2)] * count)\n    eigs = np.array(eigs, dtype=float)\n    assert len(eigs) == total_modes(rho, N), (\n        f\"eigenvalue count {len(eigs)} != total_modes({rho}, {N}) = {total_modes(rho, N)}\"\n    )\n    return eigs\n\n\ndef linear_operator(rho, N):\n    \"\"\"Diagonal linear operator for sector rho at cutoff N.\n\n    Returns a diagonal matrix of shape (m, m) where m = total_modes(rho, N),\n    with diagonal entries -n(n+2) for each basis function at level n.\n    This is the Galerkin representation of the Laplacian on S^3.\n    \"\"\"\n    eigs = laplacian_eigenvalues(rho, N)\n    return np.diag(-eigs)\n\n\ndef real_jacobian(phi, omega, rho, N, c1=1.0):\n    \"\"\"Real Jacobian of the cubic residual R(phi; omega) at state (phi, omega).\n\n    The residual is:\n        R(phi; omega) = Delta phi - c1 <phi,phi> phi + omega^2 phi\n\n    where <phi,phi> = phi^H phi (Hermitian inner product, real-valued) and\n    Delta is diagonal with entries -n(n+2) in the Galerkin basis.\n\n    Because phi -> <phi,phi> phi is NOT complex-differentiable, the Jacobian\n    is real-linear on R^{2m} (m = number of complex modes).  Writing\n    phi = x + iy and v = [x; y] in R^{2m}:\n\n        J = diag([l; l]) + omega^2 I_{2m} - c1 <phi,phi> I_{2m} - 2 c1 v v^T\n\n    where l is the vector of Laplacian eigenvalues -n(n+2).\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    phi : complex array of length m\n        Current complex state vector.\n    omega : float\n        Frequency parameter (the Jacobian depends on omega^2).\n    rho : str\n        Sector name (e.g. 'R0').\n    N : int\n        Spectral cutoff.\n    c1 : float\n        Nonlinear coupling constant (default 1.0).\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    J : ndarray of shape (2m, 2m)\n        The real Jacobian matrix.\n    \"\"\"\n    phi = np.asarray(phi, dtype=complex).ravel()\n    m = total_modes(rho, N)\n    assert len(phi) == m, f\"phi has {len(phi)} entries, expected {m}\"\n\n    l = -laplacian_eigenvalues(rho, N)          # diagonal of Laplacian: -n(n+2)\n    phi_norm_sq = np.vdot(phi, phi).real        # <phi, phi>\n    v = np.concatenate([phi.real, phi.imag])     # real representation\n\n    # Diagonal part: (L + omega^2 I - c1 <phi,phi> I) in real coords\n    diag_val = l + omega**2 - c1 * phi_norm_sq\n    diag_block = np.concatenate([diag_val, diag_val])\n    J = np.diag(diag_block)\n\n    # Rank-1 update: -2 c1 v v^T\n    J -= 2.0 * c1 * np.outer(v, v)\n\n    return J\n\n\ndef K_defect(A):\n    \"\"\"Self-adjointness defect: ||A - A^H||_2 (spectral norm of the skew part).\n\n    For a real matrix, A^H = A^T, so this measures ||A - A^T||_2.\n    \"\"\"\n    A = np.asarray(A)\n    return np.linalg.norm(A - A.conj().T, 2)\n\n\ndef J_defect(A):\n    \"\"\"Imaginary-spectrum defect: max |Im lambda(A)| over all eigenvalues.\n\n    A self-adjoint operator has purely real spectrum, so this measures\n    departure from self-adjointness in spectral terms.\n    \"\"\"\n    A = np.asarray(A)\n    eigvals = np.linalg.eigvals(A)\n    return float(np.max(np.abs(eigvals.imag)))\n","numLines":119,"startLine":1,"totalLines":119}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d6261879-21f7-42b5-99b5-68dec6899be5","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"89267438-7bdd-4b10-a91c-c1907892b4a8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CeZLn9LYCM5ZyWM3htag4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CkGVmdScAgrGC4JiUqmfST","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/sections.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4802,"cache_read_input_tokens":16388,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4802,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CeZLn8XvXci1toJRdXAUZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2f33b1ca-7872-4596-986c-dbce0870427b","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:23.689Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"2f33b1ca-7872-4596-986c-dbce0870427b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e22f4082-815d-4291-8ec0-471a24337109","agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CkGVmdScAgrGC4JiUqmfST","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Sector bases: intertwiner spaces Hom_2I(V_n, W_rho) and section bases for\n2\tall sectors at all required levels.\n3\t\n4\tThe deterministic construction per § 3: stacked-constraint SVD over the\n5\tgenerating pair, Lowdin orthonormalization against the per-level Gram, sign\n6\tfixed by the first nonzero component.\n7\t\n8\tEach section f_{i,j}(x) = A_i * pi_n(x) * e_j takes values in W_rho,\n9\twhere A_i is d_rho x (n+1), pi_n(x) is the unitarized Sym^n at point x,\n10\tand e_j is the j-th standard basis vector.\n11\t\"\"\"\n12\timport numpy as np\n13\timport hashlib\n14\tfrom scipy.linalg import sqrtm, svd as scipy_svd\n15\tfrom build.group import (G120, GEN_INDICES, IRREP_NAMES, DIMS, D_RHO,\n16\t                          multiplicity, pi_n_unitary, pi_n_mono, _sym_gram,\n17\t                          rep_rho, ALL_REPS, qmul, quat_to_su2, sym_power)\n18\t\n19\t\n20\tdef compute_intertwiners(rho, n):\n21\t    \"\"\"Compute Hom_2I(V_n, W_rho) via stacked equivariance constraints\n22\t    rho(g) A = A pi_n(g) over the generating pair, in the UNITARIZED basis.\n23\t\n24\t    Uses the CG-recurrence-based pi_n_unitary for numerical stability at all n.\n25\t\n26\t    Returns array of shape (mult, d_rho, n+1) in the UNITARIZED bases.\n27\t    \"\"\"\n28\t    d_rho = DIMS[rho]\n29\t    dim_n = n + 1\n30\t    if d_rho == 0 or dim_n == 0:\n31\t        return np.zeros((0, 0, 0), dtype=complex)\n32\t\n33\t    m = multiplicity(rho, n)\n34\t    if m == 0:\n35\t        return np.zeros((0, d_rho, dim_n), dtype=complex)\n36\t\n37\t    vec_dim = d_rho * dim_n\n38\t\n39\t    rows = []\n40\t    for gen_idx in GEN_INDICES:\n41\t        rho_g = ALL_REPS[rho][gen_idx]\n42\t        pi_g = pi_n_unitary(n, G120[gen_idx])\n43\t        C = np.kron(np.eye(dim_n), rho_g) - np.kron(pi_g.T, np.eye(d_rho))\n44\t        rows.append(C)\n45\t    M = np.vstack(rows)\n46\t\n47\t    U, S, Vh = scipy_svd(M, full_matrices=True, lapack_driver='gesvd')\n48\t    tol = max(1e-10 * S[0], 1e-12) if len(S) > 0 else 1e-12\n49\t    null_mask = S < tol\n50\t    n_null_from_sv = null_mask.sum()\n51\t    n_trailing = max(0, Vh.shape[0] - len(S))\n52\t    n_null = n_null_from_sv + n_trailing\n53\t\n54\t    assert n_null == m, f\"expected mult {m} for {rho} at n={n}, got nullity {n_null}\"\n55\t\n56\t    null_vecs = Vh[-n_null:]\n57\t    # kron uses column-major vectorization: reshape accordingly\n58\t    intertwiners = np.array([v.reshape(dim_n, d_rho).T for v in null_vecs])\n59\t\n60\t    # sign fix: first nonzero component has positive real part\n61\t    for i in range(m):\n62\t        flat = intertwiners[i].ravel()\n63\t        for c in flat:\n64\t            if abs(c) > 1e-14:\n65\t                phase = c / abs(c)\n66\t                intertwiners[i] /= phase\n67\t                break\n68\t\n69\t    return intertwiners\n70\t\n71\t\n72\tdef level_gram_analytic(n):\n73\t    \"\"\"Per-level Gram matrix for sections in the UNITARIZED basis.\n74\t\n75\t    For the unitarized representation, Schur orthogonality gives:\n76\t    integral pi_n_u(x)^H pi_n_u(x) dx = I / (n+1)\n77\t\n78\t    So for sections f_{i,j}(x) = A_i pi_n_u(x) e_j:\n79\t    <f_{i,j}, f_{k,l}> = sum_alpha,b,c [A_i^H]_{b,alpha} [A_k]_{alpha,c} delta_{jl} / (n+1)\n80\t    = delta_{jl} [A_i^H A_k]_{j...} ...\n81\t\n82\t    Actually, more carefully:\n83\t    <f_{i,j}, f_{k,l}> = integral sum_a f_{i,j,a}(x)* f_{k,l,a}(x) dx\n84\t    = integral sum_a (sum_b [A_i]_{a,b} [pi_n(x)]_{b,j})* (sum_c [A_k]_{a,c} [pi_n(x)]_{c,l}) dx\n85\t    = sum_{a,b,c} [A_i]_{a,b}* [A_k]_{a,c} integral [pi_n(x)]_{b,j}* [pi_n(x)]_{c,l} dx\n86\t\n87\t    For UNITARIZED pi_n: integral [pi_n_u]_{bj}* [pi_n_u]_{cl} dx = delta_{bc} delta_{jl} / (n+1)\n88\t\n89\t    So <f_{i,j}, f_{k,l}> = delta_{jl} / (n+1) * sum_a [A_i]_{a,j}* [A_k]_{a,j}\n90\t    Wait, that's not right. Let me redo:\n91\t    = sum_{a,b,c} [A_i*]_{a,b} [A_k]_{a,c} * delta_{bc} delta_{jl} / (n+1)\n92\t    = delta_{jl} / (n+1) * sum_{a,b} [A_i*]_{a,b} [A_k]_{a,b}\n93\t    = delta_{jl} / (n+1) * tr(A_i^H A_k)\n94\t\n95\t    Hmm, but that means the Gram in the (i,j) indices is:\n96\t    G_{(i,j),(k,l)} = delta_{jl} / (n+1) * tr(A_i^H A_k)\n97\t\n98\t    This is block-diagonal in j,l with the intertwiner overlap matrix\n99\t    H_{ik} = tr(A_i^H A_k) / (n+1) replicated (n+1) times.\n100\t\n101\t    Lowdin orthonormalization on this Gram makes the basis orthonormal.\n102\t    \"\"\"\n103\t    pass  # we compute it numerically via quadrature for safety\n104\t\n105\t\n106\tdef hopf_rule(D):\n107\t    \"\"\"Hopf-coordinate product rule exact for polynomials of degree <= D on S^3.\n108\t    Returns (nodes, weights) with weights normalized to sum to 1.\"\"\"\n109\t    K = D + 1\n110\t    nu = D // 2 + 1\n111\t    xs, ws = np.polynomial.legendre.leggauss(nu)\n112\t    u = (xs + 1) / 2\n113\t    wu = ws / 2\n114\t    xi = 2 * np.pi * np.arange(K) / K\n115\t    ce, se = np.sqrt(1 - u), np.sqrt(u)\n116\t    X, W = [], []\n117\t    for cu, su, w in zip(ce, se, wu):\n118\t        for a in xi:\n119\t            for b in xi:\n120\t                X.append([cu * np.cos(a), cu * np.sin(a),\n121\t                          su * np.cos(b), su * np.sin(b)])\n122\t                W.append(w)\n123\t    X = np.array(X)\n124\t    W = np.array(W)\n125\t    W /= W.sum()\n126\t    return X, W\n127\t\n128\t\n129\tdef eval_pi_n_unitary_at_nodes(n, nodes):\n130\t    \"\"\"Evaluate pi_n_unitary at each node. Returns array of shape (n_nodes, n+1, n+1).\"\"\"\n131\t    g = _sym_gram(n)\n132\t    Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n133\t    Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n134\t    result = np.zeros((len(nodes), n + 1, n + 1), dtype=complex)\n135\t    for i, x in enumerate(nodes):\n136\t        M = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(x, float))\n137\t        result[i] = Dh @ sym_power(M, n) @ Dih\n138\t    return result\n139\t\n140\t\n141\tdef build_basis_object(rho, N_max):\n142\t    \"\"\"Build the § 3 basis object for sector rho at all levels n = 0..N_max.\n143\t\n144\t    Returns a dict: level n -> array of shape (total_modes_at_n, d_rho)\n145\t    where total_modes_at_n = mult(rho, n) * (n+1), representing the\n146\t    section coefficients in the unitarized harmonic basis.\n147\t\n148\t    The basis is Lowdin-orthonormalized and sign-fixed per § 3's construction.\n149\t    \"\"\"\n150\t    d_rho = DIMS[rho]\n151\t    basis = {}\n152\t\n153\t    for n in range(N_max + 1):\n154\t        m = multiplicity(rho, n)\n155\t        if m == 0:\n156\t            continue\n157\t        dim_n = n + 1\n158\t        total = m * dim_n\n159\t\n160\t        # compute intertwiners (shape: m x d_rho x dim_n)\n161\t        A = compute_intertwiners(rho, n)\n162\t        assert A.shape == (m, d_rho, dim_n)\n163\t\n164\t        # Gram matrix for the section basis:\n165\t        # G_{(i,j),(k,l)} = delta_{jl} * tr(A_i^H A_k) / (n+1)\n166\t        # This is block diagonal: for each j, the m x m block is H / (n+1)\n167\t        # where H_{ik} = tr(A_i^H A_k)\n168\t        H = np.zeros((m, m), dtype=complex)\n169\t        for i in range(m):\n170\t            for k in range(m):\n171\t                H[i, k] = np.trace(A[i].conj().T @ A[k])\n172\t        H /= (n + 1)\n173\t\n174\t        # Lowdin: B = H^{-1/2}, applied to each j-block independently\n175\t        # Since H is the same for all j, this simplifies\n176\t        H_half = sqrtm(H)\n177\t        try:\n178\t            B = np.linalg.inv(H_half)\n179\t        except np.linalg.LinAlgError:\n180\t            raise RuntimeError(f\"singular Gram at {rho}, n={n}\")\n181\t\n182\t        # orthonormalized intertwiners: A_orth = sum_k B_{ik} A_k\n183\t        A_orth = np.einsum('ik,kab->iab', B, A)\n184\t\n185\t        # verify orthonormality\n186\t        H_check = np.zeros((m, m), dtype=complex)\n187\t        for i in range(m):\n188\t            for k in range(m):\n189\t                H_check[i, k] = np.trace(A_orth[i].conj().T @ A_orth[k])\n190\t        H_check /= (n + 1)\n191\t        assert np.abs(H_check - np.eye(m)).max() < 1e-10, \\\n192\t            f\"Lowdin failed at {rho}, n={n}: {np.abs(H_check - np.eye(m)).max():.2e}\"\n193\t\n194\t        # sign fix each intertwiner\n195\t        for i in range(m):\n196\t            flat = A_orth[i].ravel()\n197\t            for c in flat:\n198\t                if abs(c) > 1e-14:\n199\t                    phase = c / abs(c)\n200\t                    A_orth[i] /= phase\n201\t                    break\n202\t\n203\t        basis[n] = A_orth  # shape (m, d_rho, dim_n)\n204\t\n205\t    return basis\n206\t\n207\t\n208\tdef basis_hash(basis_obj, rho):\n209\t    \"\"\"SHA-256 of the basis object's canonical serialization.\n210\t    Coefficients as little-endian float64 (real, imag) pairs in level-major,\n211\t    then intertwiner-index, then row, then column order.\"\"\"\n212\t    h = hashlib.sha256()\n213\t    for n in sorted(basis_obj.keys()):\n214\t        A = basis_obj[n]  # (m, d_rho, dim_n)\n215\t        for i in range(A.shape[0]):\n216\t            for r in range(A.shape[1]):\n217\t                for c in range(A.shape[2]):\n218\t                    z = A[i, r, c]\n219\t                    h.update(np.float64(z.real).tobytes())\n220\t                    h.update(np.float64(z.imag).tobytes())\n221\t    return h.hexdigest()\n222\t\n223\t\n224\tdef total_modes(rho, N):\n225\t    \"\"\"Total complex Galerkin modes in sector rho at cutoff N.\"\"\"\n226\t    return sum(multiplicity(rho, n) * (n + 1) for n in range(N + 1))\n227\t"}]},"uuid":"dbfccac8-d12f-427c-a154-8c4b7955f495","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:23.693Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/sections.py","content":"\"\"\"Sector bases: intertwiner spaces Hom_2I(V_n, W_rho) and section bases for\nall sectors at all required levels.\n\nThe deterministic construction per § 3: stacked-constraint SVD over the\ngenerating pair, Lowdin orthonormalization against the per-level Gram, sign\nfixed by the first nonzero component.\n\nEach section f_{i,j}(x) = A_i * pi_n(x) * e_j takes values in W_rho,\nwhere A_i is d_rho x (n+1), pi_n(x) is the unitarized Sym^n at point x,\nand e_j is the j-th standard basis vector.\n\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nimport hashlib\nfrom scipy.linalg import sqrtm, svd as scipy_svd\nfrom build.group import (G120, GEN_INDICES, IRREP_NAMES, DIMS, D_RHO,\n                          multiplicity, pi_n_unitary, pi_n_mono, _sym_gram,\n                          rep_rho, ALL_REPS, qmul, quat_to_su2, sym_power)\n\n\ndef compute_intertwiners(rho, n):\n    \"\"\"Compute Hom_2I(V_n, W_rho) via stacked equivariance constraints\n    rho(g) A = A pi_n(g) over the generating pair, in the UNITARIZED basis.\n\n    Uses the CG-recurrence-based pi_n_unitary for numerical stability at all n.\n\n    Returns array of shape (mult, d_rho, n+1) in the UNITARIZED bases.\n    \"\"\"\n    d_rho = DIMS[rho]\n    dim_n = n + 1\n    if d_rho == 0 or dim_n == 0:\n        return np.zeros((0, 0, 0), dtype=complex)\n\n    m = multiplicity(rho, n)\n    if m == 0:\n        return np.zeros((0, d_rho, dim_n), dtype=complex)\n\n    vec_dim = d_rho * dim_n\n\n    rows = []\n    for gen_idx in GEN_INDICES:\n        rho_g = ALL_REPS[rho][gen_idx]\n        pi_g = pi_n_unitary(n, G120[gen_idx])\n        C = np.kron(np.eye(dim_n), rho_g) - np.kron(pi_g.T, np.eye(d_rho))\n        rows.append(C)\n    M = np.vstack(rows)\n\n    U, S, Vh = scipy_svd(M, full_matrices=True, lapack_driver='gesvd')\n    tol = max(1e-10 * S[0], 1e-12) if len(S) > 0 else 1e-12\n    null_mask = S < tol\n    n_null_from_sv = null_mask.sum()\n    n_trailing = max(0, Vh.shape[0] - len(S))\n    n_null = n_null_from_sv + n_trailing\n\n    assert n_null == m, f\"expected mult {m} for {rho} at n={n}, got nullity {n_null}\"\n\n    null_vecs = Vh[-n_null:]\n    # kron uses column-major vectorization: reshape accordingly\n    intertwiners = np.array([v.reshape(dim_n, d_rho).T for v in null_vecs])\n\n    # sign fix: first nonzero component has positive real part\n    for i in range(m):\n        flat = intertwiners[i].ravel()\n        for c in flat:\n            if abs(c) > 1e-14:\n                phase = c / abs(c)\n                intertwiners[i] /= phase\n                break\n\n    return intertwiners\n\n\ndef level_gram_analytic(n):\n    \"\"\"Per-level Gram matrix for sections in the UNITARIZED basis.\n\n    For the unitarized representation, Schur orthogonality gives:\n    integral pi_n_u(x)^H pi_n_u(x) dx = I / (n+1)\n\n    So for sections f_{i,j}(x) = A_i pi_n_u(x) e_j:\n    <f_{i,j}, f_{k,l}> = sum_alpha,b,c [A_i^H]_{b,alpha} [A_k]_{alpha,c} delta_{jl} / (n+1)\n    = delta_{jl} [A_i^H A_k]_{j...} ...\n\n    Actually, more carefully:\n    <f_{i,j}, f_{k,l}> = integral sum_a f_{i,j,a}(x)* f_{k,l,a}(x) dx\n    = integral sum_a (sum_b [A_i]_{a,b} [pi_n(x)]_{b,j})* (sum_c [A_k]_{a,c} [pi_n(x)]_{c,l}) dx\n    = sum_{a,b,c} [A_i]_{a,b}* [A_k]_{a,c} integral [pi_n(x)]_{b,j}* [pi_n(x)]_{c,l} dx\n\n    For UNITARIZED pi_n: integral [pi_n_u]_{bj}* [pi_n_u]_{cl} dx = delta_{bc} delta_{jl} / (n+1)\n\n    So <f_{i,j}, f_{k,l}> = delta_{jl} / (n+1) * sum_a [A_i]_{a,j}* [A_k]_{a,j}\n    Wait, that's not right. Let me redo:\n    = sum_{a,b,c} [A_i*]_{a,b} [A_k]_{a,c} * delta_{bc} delta_{jl} / (n+1)\n    = delta_{jl} / (n+1) * sum_{a,b} [A_i*]_{a,b} [A_k]_{a,b}\n    = delta_{jl} / (n+1) * tr(A_i^H A_k)\n\n    Hmm, but that means the Gram in the (i,j) indices is:\n    G_{(i,j),(k,l)} = delta_{jl} / (n+1) * tr(A_i^H A_k)\n\n    This is block-diagonal in j,l with the intertwiner overlap matrix\n    H_{ik} = tr(A_i^H A_k) / (n+1) replicated (n+1) times.\n\n    Lowdin orthonormalization on this Gram makes the basis orthonormal.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass  # we compute it numerically via quadrature for safety\n\n\ndef hopf_rule(D):\n    \"\"\"Hopf-coordinate product rule exact for polynomials of degree <= D on S^3.\n    Returns (nodes, weights) with weights normalized to sum to 1.\"\"\"\n    K = D + 1\n    nu = D // 2 + 1\n    xs, ws = np.polynomial.legendre.leggauss(nu)\n    u = (xs + 1) / 2\n    wu = ws / 2\n    xi = 2 * np.pi * np.arange(K) / K\n    ce, se = np.sqrt(1 - u), np.sqrt(u)\n    X, W = [], []\n    for cu, su, w in zip(ce, se, wu):\n        for a in xi:\n            for b in xi:\n                X.append([cu * np.cos(a), cu * np.sin(a),\n                          su * np.cos(b), su * np.sin(b)])\n                W.append(w)\n    X = np.array(X)\n    W = np.array(W)\n    W /= W.sum()\n    return X, W\n\n\ndef eval_pi_n_unitary_at_nodes(n, nodes):\n    \"\"\"Evaluate pi_n_unitary at each node. Returns array of shape (n_nodes, n+1, n+1).\"\"\"\n    g = _sym_gram(n)\n    Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n    Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n    result = np.zeros((len(nodes), n + 1, n + 1), dtype=complex)\n    for i, x in enumerate(nodes):\n        M = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(x, float))\n        result[i] = Dh @ sym_power(M, n) @ Dih\n    return result\n\n\ndef build_basis_object(rho, N_max):\n    \"\"\"Build the § 3 basis object for sector rho at all levels n = 0..N_max.\n\n    Returns a dict: level n -> array of shape (total_modes_at_n, d_rho)\n    where total_modes_at_n = mult(rho, n) * (n+1), representing the\n    section coefficients in the unitarized harmonic basis.\n\n    The basis is Lowdin-orthonormalized and sign-fixed per § 3's construction.\n    \"\"\"\n    d_rho = DIMS[rho]\n    basis = {}\n\n    for n in range(N_max + 1):\n        m = multiplicity(rho, n)\n        if m == 0:\n            continue\n        dim_n = n + 1\n        total = m * dim_n\n\n        # compute intertwiners (shape: m x d_rho x dim_n)\n        A = compute_intertwiners(rho, n)\n        assert A.shape == (m, d_rho, dim_n)\n\n        # Gram matrix for the section basis:\n        # G_{(i,j),(k,l)} = delta_{jl} * tr(A_i^H A_k) / (n+1)\n        # This is block diagonal: for each j, the m x m block is H / (n+1)\n        # where H_{ik} = tr(A_i^H A_k)\n        H = np.zeros((m, m), dtype=complex)\n        for i in range(m):\n            for k in range(m):\n                H[i, k] = np.trace(A[i].conj().T @ A[k])\n        H /= (n + 1)\n\n        # Lowdin: B = H^{-1/2}, applied to each j-block independently\n        # Since H is the same for all j, this simplifies\n        H_half = sqrtm(H)\n        try:\n            B = np.linalg.inv(H_half)\n        except np.linalg.LinAlgError:\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"singular Gram at {rho}, n={n}\")\n\n        # orthonormalized intertwiners: A_orth = sum_k B_{ik} A_k\n        A_orth = np.einsum('ik,kab->iab', B, A)\n\n        # verify orthonormality\n        H_check = np.zeros((m, m), dtype=complex)\n        for i in range(m):\n            for k in range(m):\n                H_check[i, k] = np.trace(A_orth[i].conj().T @ A_orth[k])\n        H_check /= (n + 1)\n        assert np.abs(H_check - np.eye(m)).max() < 1e-10, \\\n            f\"Lowdin failed at {rho}, n={n}: {np.abs(H_check - np.eye(m)).max():.2e}\"\n\n        # sign fix each intertwiner\n        for i in range(m):\n            flat = A_orth[i].ravel()\n            for c in flat:\n                if abs(c) > 1e-14:\n                    phase = c / abs(c)\n                    A_orth[i] /= phase\n                    break\n\n        basis[n] = A_orth  # shape (m, d_rho, dim_n)\n\n    return basis\n\n\ndef basis_hash(basis_obj, rho):\n    \"\"\"SHA-256 of the basis object's canonical serialization.\n    Coefficients as little-endian float64 (real, imag) pairs in level-major,\n    then intertwiner-index, then row, then column order.\"\"\"\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    for n in sorted(basis_obj.keys()):\n        A = basis_obj[n]  # (m, d_rho, dim_n)\n        for i in range(A.shape[0]):\n            for r in range(A.shape[1]):\n                for c in range(A.shape[2]):\n                    z = A[i, r, c]\n                    h.update(np.float64(z.real).tobytes())\n                    h.update(np.float64(z.imag).tobytes())\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef total_modes(rho, N):\n    \"\"\"Total complex Galerkin modes in sector rho at cutoff N.\"\"\"\n    return sum(multiplicity(rho, n) * (n + 1) for n in range(N + 1))\n","numLines":227,"startLine":1,"totalLines":227}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2f33b1ca-7872-4596-986c-dbce0870427b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"dbfccac8-d12f-427c-a154-8c4b7955f495","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CeZLn9LYCM5ZyWM3htag4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W3Ra5K1suoUcuUUxwxTo6X","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/group.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4802,"cache_read_input_tokens":16388,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4802,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CeZLn8XvXci1toJRdXAUZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4742938d-57f0-48db-9d40-e56f4fbcc1e8","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:24.236Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"4742938d-57f0-48db-9d40-e56f4fbcc1e8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e22f4082-815d-4291-8ec0-471a24337109","agentId":"a9164c81ffbd93f27","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W3Ra5K1suoUcuUUxwxTo6X","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Binary icosahedral group 2I: 120 unit quaternions, explicit construction,\n2\tcharacter table, generating pair, and unitary representations for all 9 sectors.\n3\t\n4\tRepresentations R1, R3, R6, R7, R8 are unitarized symmetric powers of the\n5\tSU(2) fundamental. R2 is extracted from V_7 via the Reynolds projector (first\n6\toccurrence of R2 is at level 7). R4 = Sym^2(R2) unitarized. R5 = R1 x R2.\n7\t\n8\tAll constructions match right_translation_check.py and mode_count.py, verified\n9\tat import time.\n10\t\"\"\"\n11\timport numpy as np\n12\timport itertools\n13\tfrom math import factorial\n14\tfrom scipy.linalg import sqrtm\n15\t\n16\tTAU = (1 + np.sqrt(5)) / 2\n17\tTAU_INV = (np.sqrt(5) - 1) / 2\n18\t\n19\t# ---- quaternion arithmetic ----\n20\tdef qmul(a, b):\n21\t    w1, x1, y1, z1 = a\n22\t    w2, x2, y2, z2 = b\n23\t    return np.array([\n24\t        w1*w2 - x1*x2 - y1*y2 - z1*z2,\n25\t        w1*x2 + x1*w2 + y1*z2 - z1*y2,\n26\t        w1*y2 - x1*z2 + y1*w2 + z1*x2,\n27\t        w1*z2 + x1*y2 - y1*x2 + z1*w2,\n28\t    ])\n29\t\n30\tdef quat_to_su2(q):\n31\t    w, x, y, z = q\n32\t    return np.array([[w + 1j*x, y + 1j*z],\n33\t                     [-y + 1j*z, w - 1j*x]], dtype=complex)\n34\t\n35\tdef sym_power(M, n):\n36\t    dim = n + 1\n37\t    out = np.zeros((dim, dim), dtype=complex)\n38\t    for k in range(dim):\n39\t        polyA = np.array([1.0 + 0j])\n40\t        for _ in range(n - k):\n41\t            polyA = np.convolve(polyA, np.array([M[0, 0], M[1, 0]]))\n42\t        polyB = np.array([1.0 + 0j])\n43\t        for _ in range(k):\n44\t            polyB = np.convolve(polyB, np.array([M[0, 1], M[1, 1]]))\n45\t        out[:, k] = np.convolve(polyA, polyB)\n46\t    return out\n47\t\n48\t# ---- group construction (matches right_translation_check.py) ----\n49\tdef _build_icosians():\n50\t    Q = []\n51\t    for i in range(4):\n52\t        for s in (1.0, -1.0):\n53\t            q = np.zeros(4); q[i] = s; Q.append(q)\n54\t    for signs in range(16):\n55\t        Q.append(np.array([0.5 if (signs >> k) & 1 == 0 else -0.5 for k in range(4)]))\n56\t    base = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 1/TAU, TAU]) / 2\n57\t    evens = [p for p in itertools.permutations(range(4))\n58\t             if sum(1 for a in range(4) for b in range(a+1, 4) if p[a] > p[b]) % 2 == 0]\n59\t    for p in evens:\n60\t        for signs in range(8):\n61\t            sb = np.array([1.0, (-1)**(signs & 1), (-1)**((signs >> 1) & 1),\n62\t                           (-1)**((signs >> 2) & 1)])\n63\t            v = base * sb\n64\t            Q.append(np.array([v[p.index(k)] for k in range(4)]))\n65\t    return np.array(Q)\n66\t\n67\tG120 = _build_icosians()\n68\tassert G120.shape == (120, 4)\n69\t\n70\tdef order_of(q):\n71\t    p = q.copy()\n72\t    for k in range(1, 12):\n73\t        if np.abs(p - np.array([1, 0, 0, 0])).sum() < 1e-9:\n74\t            return k\n75\t        p = qmul(p, q)\n76\t    return -1\n77\t\n78\t# ---- verification ----\n79\t_unique_count = len({tuple(np.round(q, 9)) for q in G120})\n80\tassert _unique_count == 120\n81\tassert np.allclose(np.einsum('ij,ij->i', G120, G120), 1.0)\n82\t_order_census = {}\n83\tfor q in G120:\n84\t    o = order_of(q)\n85\t    _order_census[o] = _order_census.get(o, 0) + 1\n86\tassert _order_census == {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 20, 4: 30, 5: 24, 6: 20, 10: 24}\n87\t\n88\tdef _closure_residual(G):\n89\t    maxres = 0.0\n90\t    for i in range(len(G)):\n91\t        for j in range(len(G)):\n92\t            p = qmul(G[i], G[j])\n93\t            dists = np.sqrt(((G - p)**2).sum(axis=1))\n94\t            maxres = max(maxres, dists.min())\n95\t    return maxres\n96\t\n97\t_cres = _closure_residual(G120)\n98\tassert _cres < 1e-9\n99\t\n100\t# ---- generating pair ----\n101\tdef _find_generators():\n102\t    ord10s = [i for i, q in enumerate(G120) if order_of(q) == 10]\n103\t    ord4s = [i for i, q in enumerate(G120) if order_of(q) == 4]\n104\t    def generate(seed_idx):\n105\t        S = {tuple(np.round(G120[i], 9)) for i in seed_idx}\n106\t        frontier = [G120[i] for i in seed_idx]\n107\t        while frontier:\n108\t            nxt = []\n109\t            for a in frontier:\n110\t                for i in seed_idx:\n111\t                    p = qmul(a, G120[i])\n112\t                    tp = tuple(np.round(p, 9))\n113\t                    if tp not in S:\n114\t                        S.add(tp)\n115\t                        nxt.append(p)\n116\t            frontier = nxt\n117\t        return S\n118\t    for a in ord10s[:3]:\n119\t        for b in ord4s:\n120\t            if len(generate([a, b])) == 120:\n121\t                return [a, b]\n122\t    raise RuntimeError(\"no generating pair found\")\n123\t\n124\tGEN_INDICES = _find_generators()\n125\t\n126\t# ---- conjugacy class snapping ----\n127\tCLASS_ANGLES = np.array([0, np.pi, np.pi/2, np.pi/3, 2*np.pi/3,\n128\t                         np.pi/5, 2*np.pi/5, 3*np.pi/5, 4*np.pi/5])\n129\tCLASS_SIZES = np.array([1, 1, 30, 20, 20, 12, 12, 12, 12])\n130\t\n131\tdef snap_class(q):\n132\t    th = np.arccos(np.clip(q[0], -1, 1))\n133\t    k = int(np.argmin(np.abs(CLASS_ANGLES - th)))\n134\t    assert abs(CLASS_ANGLES[k] - th) < 1e-9, f\"angle {th} off every 2I class\"\n135\t    return k\n136\t\n137\tELEMENT_CLASSES = np.array([snap_class(q) for q in G120])\n138\t\n139\t# ---- character table ----\n140\tGALOIS_CLASS = {5: 7, 7: 5, 6: 8, 8: 6}\n141\t\n142\tdef chiV(n, k):\n143\t    th = CLASS_ANGLES[k]\n144\t    if k == 0:\n145\t        return float(n + 1)\n146\t    if k == 1:\n147\t        return float((n + 1) * (-1)**n)\n148\t    return np.sin((n + 1) * th) / np.sin(th)\n149\t\n150\tIRREP_NAMES = [\"R0\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R3\", \"R4\", \"R5\", \"R6\", \"R7\", \"R8\"]\n151\t\n152\tdef _build_chartab():\n153\t    IRREPS8 = {\n154\t        \"R0\": lambda k: 1.0,\n155\t        \"R1\": lambda k: chiV(1, k),\n156\t        \"R2\": lambda k: chiV(1, GALOIS_CLASS.get(k, k)),\n157\t        \"R3\": lambda k: chiV(2, k),\n158\t        \"R4\": lambda k: chiV(2, GALOIS_CLASS.get(k, k)),\n159\t        \"R6\": lambda k: chiV(3, k),\n160\t        \"R7\": lambda k: chiV(4, k),\n161\t        \"R8\": lambda k: chiV(5, k),\n162\t    }\n163\t    others_vec = {r: np.array([IRREPS8[r](k) for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES]) for r in IRREPS8}\n164\t    import collections\n165\t    csize = collections.Counter(ELEMENT_CLASSES)\n166\t    cent = {k: 120 // csize[k] for k in csize}\n167\t    absq = {k: cent[k] - sum(IRREPS8[r](k)**2 for r in IRREPS8) for k in csize}\n168\t    amb = [k for k in sorted(csize) if absq[k] > 0.5 and k != 0]\n169\t    hits = []\n170\t    for bits in range(2 ** len(amb)):\n171\t        row = {k: float(np.sqrt(max(absq[k], 0.0))) for k in csize}\n172\t        for j, k in enumerate(amb):\n173\t            if (bits >> j) & 1:\n174\t                row[k] = -row[k]\n175\t        vec = np.array([row[k] for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES])\n176\t        if (all(abs(np.mean(vec * others_vec[r])) < 1e-9 for r in IRREPS8)\n177\t                and abs(np.mean(vec * vec) - 1.0) < 1e-9):\n178\t            hits.append(vec)\n179\t    assert len(hits) == 1, f\"R5 sign solution not unique: {len(hits)}\"\n180\t    tab = {r: others_vec[r] for r in IRREPS8}\n181\t    tab[\"R5\"] = hits[0]\n182\t    return tab\n183\t\n184\tCHARTAB = _build_chartab()\n185\t\n186\t_orth = np.array([[np.mean(CHARTAB[a] * CHARTAB[b]) for b in IRREP_NAMES]\n187\t                  for a in IRREP_NAMES])\n188\tassert np.abs(_orth - np.eye(9)).max() < 1e-9\n189\t\n190\tDIMS = {name: int(round(CHARTAB[name][0])) for name in IRREP_NAMES}\n191\tD_RHO = {\"R0\": 0, \"R1\": 1, \"R3\": 2, \"R6\": 3, \"R7\": 4, \"R8\": 5, \"R4\": 6, \"R5\": 6, \"R2\": 7}\n192\t\n193\tdef multiplicity(rho, n):\n194\t    return int(round(np.mean(np.array([chiV(n, k) for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES]) * CHARTAB[rho])))\n195\t\n196\t# ---- unitarization of symmetric powers ----\n197\tdef _sym_gram(n):\n198\t    \"\"\"Diagonal Gram for Sym^n in monomial basis: G_kk = k!(n-k)!/n!\"\"\"\n199\t    fn = factorial(n)\n200\t    return np.array([factorial(k) * factorial(n - k) / fn for k in range(n + 1)])\n201\t\n202\tdef pi_n_mono(n, q):\n203\t    if n == 0:\n204\t        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n205\t    return sym_power(quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float)), n)\n206\t\n207\tdef _pi_n_cg_recurrence(n, U):\n208\t    \"\"\"Sym^n in unitarized (weight) basis via CG recurrence. Stable at all n.\"\"\"\n209\t    D = U.copy()\n210\t    for step in range(1, n):\n211\t        dim_old = step + 1\n212\t        dim_new = step + 2\n213\t        D_pad = np.zeros((dim_new, dim_new), dtype=complex)\n214\t        D_pad[:dim_old, :dim_old] = D\n215\t        D_pm = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n216\t        D_pm[:, 1:] = D_pad[:, :dim_new - 1]\n217\t        D_mp = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n218\t        D_mp[1:, :] = D_pad[:dim_new - 1, :]\n219\t        D_mm = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n220\t        D_mm[1:, 1:] = D_pad[:dim_new - 1, :dim_new - 1]\n221\t        i_arr = np.arange(dim_new, dtype=float)\n222\t        cg_op = np.sqrt(np.clip((dim_old - i_arr) / dim_old, 0, None))\n223\t        cg_om = np.sqrt(np.clip(i_arr / dim_old, 0, None))\n224\t        CG_pp = cg_op[:, None] * cg_op[None, :]\n225\t        CG_pm = cg_op[:, None] * cg_om[None, :]\n226\t        CG_mp = cg_om[:, None] * cg_op[None, :]\n227\t        CG_mm = cg_om[:, None] * cg_om[None, :]\n228\t        D = (CG_pp * D_pad * U[0, 0] + CG_pm * D_pm * U[0, 1] +\n229\t             CG_mp * D_mp * U[1, 0] + CG_mm * D_mm * U[1, 1])\n230\t    return D\n231\t\n232\tdef pi_n_unitary(n, q):\n233\t    if n == 0:\n234\t        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n235\t    U = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n236\t    if n <= 40:\n237\t        g = _sym_gram(n)\n238\t        Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n239\t        Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n240\t        return Dh @ sym_power(U, n) @ Dih\n241\t    return _pi_n_cg_recurrence(n, U)\n242\t\n243\t# ---- extract R2 from V_7 via Reynolds projector ----\n244\tdef _build_R2_matrices():\n245\t    \"\"\"Extract R2 (dim 2) from V_7|_{2I} using the character-weighted Reynolds projector.\"\"\"\n246\t    d = 8  # dim V_7\n247\t    all_V7 = np.zeros((120, d, d), dtype=complex)\n248\t    for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n249\t        all_V7[i] = pi_n_unitary(7, q)\n250\t    chi_R2 = CHARTAB[\"R2\"]\n251\t    P = np.zeros((d, d), dtype=complex)\n252\t    for i in range(120):\n253\t        P += chi_R2[i].conj() * all_V7[i]\n254\t    P *= 2.0 / 120.0\n255\t    eigvals, eigvecs = np.linalg.eigh(P)\n256\t    mask = eigvals > 0.5\n257\t    assert mask.sum() == 2, f\"expected rank 2, got {mask.sum()}\"\n258\t    U = eigvecs[:, mask]  # 8x2\n259\t    R2_mats = np.zeros((120, 2, 2), dtype=complex)\n260\t    for i in range(120):\n261\t        R2_mats[i] = U.conj().T @ all_V7[i] @ U\n262\t    return R2_mats\n263\t\n264\t_R2_ALL = _build_R2_matrices()\n265\t\n266\t# ---- build R4 = Sym^2(R2) ----\n267\tdef _sym2_of_mat(M):\n268\t    \"\"\"Sym^2 of a 2x2 matrix in the monomial basis {u^2, uv, v^2}.\"\"\"\n269\t    return sym_power(M, 2)\n270\t\n271\tdef _build_R4_matrices():\n272\t    \"\"\"R4 = unitarized Sym^2(R2).\n273\t    R2 is already unitary (det 1), so Sym^2(R2) uses the same Gram as Sym^2(SU2).\"\"\"\n274\t    g = _sym_gram(2)\n275\t    Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n276\t    Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n277\t    R4_mats = np.zeros((120, 3, 3), dtype=complex)\n278\t    for i in range(120):\n279\t        R4_mats[i] = Dh @ _sym2_of_mat(_R2_ALL[i]) @ Dih\n280\t    return R4_mats\n281\t\n282\t_R4_ALL = _build_R4_matrices()\n283\t\n284\t# ---- build R5 = R1 tensor R2 ----\n285\tdef _build_R5_matrices():\n286\t    R5_mats = np.zeros((120, 4, 4), dtype=complex)\n287\t    for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n288\t        R1 = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n289\t        R5_mats[i] = np.kron(R1, _R2_ALL[i])\n290\t    return R5_mats\n291\t\n292\t_R5_ALL = _build_R5_matrices()\n293\t\n294\t# ---- precompute all rep matrices ----\n295\tdef _precompute_all():\n296\t    \"\"\"Precompute all 120 matrices for every sector.\"\"\"\n297\t    all_reps = {}\n298\t    for rho in IRREP_NAMES:\n299\t        d = DIMS[rho]\n300\t        mats = np.zeros((120, d, d), dtype=complex)\n301\t        for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n302\t            mats[i] = _rep_rho_single(rho, i)\n303\t        all_reps[rho] = mats\n304\t    return all_reps\n305\t\n306\tdef _rep_rho_single(rho, idx):\n307\t    q = G120[idx]\n308\t    if rho == \"R0\":\n309\t        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n310\t    elif rho == \"R1\":\n311\t        return quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n312\t    elif rho == \"R2\":\n313\t        return _R2_ALL[idx]\n314\t    elif rho == \"R3\":\n315\t        return pi_n_unitary(2, q)\n316\t    elif rho == \"R4\":\n317\t        return _R4_ALL[idx]\n318\t    elif rho == \"R5\":\n319\t        return _R5_ALL[idx]\n320\t    elif rho == \"R6\":\n321\t        return pi_n_unitary(3, q)\n322\t    elif rho == \"R7\":\n323\t        return pi_n_unitary(4, q)\n324\t    elif rho == \"R8\":\n325\t        return pi_n_unitary(5, q)\n326\t    raise ValueError(f\"unknown sector: {rho}\")\n327\t\n328\tALL_REPS = _precompute_all()\n329\t\n330\tdef rep_rho(rho, idx):\n331\t    \"\"\"Unitary representation matrix of G120[idx] in sector rho.\"\"\"\n332\t    return ALL_REPS[rho][idx]\n333\t\n334\tdef rep_rho_quat(rho, q):\n335\t    \"\"\"Unitary representation matrix of quaternion q in sector rho.\n336\t    q must be in G120 (found by nearest-neighbor lookup).\"\"\"\n337\t    dists = np.sqrt(((G120 - q)**2).sum(axis=1))\n338\t    idx = int(np.argmin(dists))\n339\t    assert dists[idx] < 1e-9, f\"quaternion not in 2I\"\n340\t    return ALL_REPS[rho][idx]\n341\t\n342\t# ---- verification ----\n343\tdef _verify_reps():\n344\t    for rho in IRREP_NAMES:\n345\t        d = DIMS[rho]\n346\t        mats = ALL_REPS[rho]\n347\t        # unitarity\n348\t        for i in range(120):\n349\t            M = mats[i]\n350\t            assert M.shape == (d, d)\n351\t            assert np.abs(M @ M.conj().T - np.eye(d)).max() < 1e-9, \\\n352\t                f\"{rho}[{i}]: not unitary\"\n353\t        # character\n354\t        for i in range(120):\n355\t            tr = np.trace(mats[i]).real\n356\t            expected = CHARTAB[rho][i]\n357\t            assert abs(tr - expected) < 1e-7, \\\n358\t                f\"{rho}[{i}]: trace {tr} != {expected}\"\n359\t        # homomorphism on full group\n360\t        for _ in range(50):\n361\t            a, b = np.random.randint(0, 120, 2)\n362\t            p = qmul(G120[a], G120[b])\n363\t            pidx = int(np.argmin(np.sqrt(((G120 - p)**2).sum(axis=1))))\n364\t            err = np.abs(mats[a] @ mats[b] - mats[pidx]).max()\n365\t            assert err < 1e-9, f\"{rho}: homomorphism error {err}\"\n366\t        # rho(-1) = +-I\n367\t        minus1_idx = int(np.argmin(np.sqrt(((G120 - np.array([-1, 0, 0, 0]))**2).sum(axis=1))))\n368\t        Mm = mats[minus1_idx]\n369\t        assert np.abs(np.abs(Mm) - np.eye(d)).max() < 1e-9, f\"{rho}: rho(-1) not +-I\"\n370\t\n371\t_verify_reps()\n372\t"}]},"uuid":"3c53c6c7-46bd-4c36-b632-a7259bf8f5cb","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:39:24.242Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/group.py","content":"\"\"\"Binary icosahedral group 2I: 120 unit quaternions, explicit construction,\ncharacter table, generating pair, and unitary representations for all 9 sectors.\n\nRepresentations R1, R3, R6, R7, R8 are unitarized symmetric powers of the\nSU(2) fundamental. R2 is extracted from V_7 via the Reynolds projector (first\noccurrence of R2 is at level 7). R4 = Sym^2(R2) unitarized. R5 = R1 x R2.\n\nAll constructions match right_translation_check.py and mode_count.py, verified\nat import time.\n\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nimport itertools\nfrom math import factorial\nfrom scipy.linalg import sqrtm\n\nTAU = (1 + np.sqrt(5)) / 2\nTAU_INV = (np.sqrt(5) - 1) / 2\n\n# ---- quaternion arithmetic ----\ndef qmul(a, b):\n    w1, x1, y1, z1 = a\n    w2, x2, y2, z2 = b\n    return np.array([\n        w1*w2 - x1*x2 - y1*y2 - z1*z2,\n        w1*x2 + x1*w2 + y1*z2 - z1*y2,\n        w1*y2 - x1*z2 + y1*w2 + z1*x2,\n        w1*z2 + x1*y2 - y1*x2 + z1*w2,\n    ])\n\ndef quat_to_su2(q):\n    w, x, y, z = q\n    return np.array([[w + 1j*x, y + 1j*z],\n                     [-y + 1j*z, w - 1j*x]], dtype=complex)\n\ndef sym_power(M, n):\n    dim = n + 1\n    out = np.zeros((dim, dim), dtype=complex)\n    for k in range(dim):\n        polyA = np.array([1.0 + 0j])\n        for _ in range(n - k):\n            polyA = np.convolve(polyA, np.array([M[0, 0], M[1, 0]]))\n        polyB = np.array([1.0 + 0j])\n        for _ in range(k):\n            polyB = np.convolve(polyB, np.array([M[0, 1], M[1, 1]]))\n        out[:, k] = np.convolve(polyA, polyB)\n    return out\n\n# ---- group construction (matches right_translation_check.py) ----\ndef _build_icosians():\n    Q = []\n    for i in range(4):\n        for s in (1.0, -1.0):\n            q = np.zeros(4); q[i] = s; Q.append(q)\n    for signs in range(16):\n        Q.append(np.array([0.5 if (signs >> k) & 1 == 0 else -0.5 for k in range(4)]))\n    base = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 1/TAU, TAU]) / 2\n    evens = [p for p in itertools.permutations(range(4))\n             if sum(1 for a in range(4) for b in range(a+1, 4) if p[a] > p[b]) % 2 == 0]\n    for p in evens:\n        for signs in range(8):\n            sb = np.array([1.0, (-1)**(signs & 1), (-1)**((signs >> 1) & 1),\n                           (-1)**((signs >> 2) & 1)])\n            v = base * sb\n            Q.append(np.array([v[p.index(k)] for k in range(4)]))\n    return np.array(Q)\n\nG120 = _build_icosians()\nassert G120.shape == (120, 4)\n\ndef order_of(q):\n    p = q.copy()\n    for k in range(1, 12):\n        if np.abs(p - np.array([1, 0, 0, 0])).sum() < 1e-9:\n            return k\n        p = qmul(p, q)\n    return -1\n\n# ---- verification ----\n_unique_count = len({tuple(np.round(q, 9)) for q in G120})\nassert _unique_count == 120\nassert np.allclose(np.einsum('ij,ij->i', G120, G120), 1.0)\n_order_census = {}\nfor q in G120:\n    o = order_of(q)\n    _order_census[o] = _order_census.get(o, 0) + 1\nassert _order_census == {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 20, 4: 30, 5: 24, 6: 20, 10: 24}\n\ndef _closure_residual(G):\n    maxres = 0.0\n    for i in range(len(G)):\n        for j in range(len(G)):\n            p = qmul(G[i], G[j])\n            dists = np.sqrt(((G - p)**2).sum(axis=1))\n            maxres = max(maxres, dists.min())\n    return maxres\n\n_cres = _closure_residual(G120)\nassert _cres < 1e-9\n\n# ---- generating pair ----\ndef _find_generators():\n    ord10s = [i for i, q in enumerate(G120) if order_of(q) == 10]\n    ord4s = [i for i, q in enumerate(G120) if order_of(q) == 4]\n    def generate(seed_idx):\n        S = {tuple(np.round(G120[i], 9)) for i in seed_idx}\n        frontier = [G120[i] for i in seed_idx]\n        while frontier:\n            nxt = []\n            for a in frontier:\n                for i in seed_idx:\n                    p = qmul(a, G120[i])\n                    tp = tuple(np.round(p, 9))\n                    if tp not in S:\n                        S.add(tp)\n                        nxt.append(p)\n            frontier = nxt\n        return S\n    for a in ord10s[:3]:\n        for b in ord4s:\n            if len(generate([a, b])) == 120:\n                return [a, b]\n    raise RuntimeError(\"no generating pair found\")\n\nGEN_INDICES = _find_generators()\n\n# ---- conjugacy class snapping ----\nCLASS_ANGLES = np.array([0, np.pi, np.pi/2, np.pi/3, 2*np.pi/3,\n                         np.pi/5, 2*np.pi/5, 3*np.pi/5, 4*np.pi/5])\nCLASS_SIZES = np.array([1, 1, 30, 20, 20, 12, 12, 12, 12])\n\ndef snap_class(q):\n    th = np.arccos(np.clip(q[0], -1, 1))\n    k = int(np.argmin(np.abs(CLASS_ANGLES - th)))\n    assert abs(CLASS_ANGLES[k] - th) < 1e-9, f\"angle {th} off every 2I class\"\n    return k\n\nELEMENT_CLASSES = np.array([snap_class(q) for q in G120])\n\n# ---- character table ----\nGALOIS_CLASS = {5: 7, 7: 5, 6: 8, 8: 6}\n\ndef chiV(n, k):\n    th = CLASS_ANGLES[k]\n    if k == 0:\n        return float(n + 1)\n    if k == 1:\n        return float((n + 1) * (-1)**n)\n    return np.sin((n + 1) * th) / np.sin(th)\n\nIRREP_NAMES = [\"R0\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R3\", \"R4\", \"R5\", \"R6\", \"R7\", \"R8\"]\n\ndef _build_chartab():\n    IRREPS8 = {\n        \"R0\": lambda k: 1.0,\n        \"R1\": lambda k: chiV(1, k),\n        \"R2\": lambda k: chiV(1, GALOIS_CLASS.get(k, k)),\n        \"R3\": lambda k: chiV(2, k),\n        \"R4\": lambda k: chiV(2, GALOIS_CLASS.get(k, k)),\n        \"R6\": lambda k: chiV(3, k),\n        \"R7\": lambda k: chiV(4, k),\n        \"R8\": lambda k: chiV(5, k),\n    }\n    others_vec = {r: np.array([IRREPS8[r](k) for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES]) for r in IRREPS8}\n    import collections\n    csize = collections.Counter(ELEMENT_CLASSES)\n    cent = {k: 120 // csize[k] for k in csize}\n    absq = {k: cent[k] - sum(IRREPS8[r](k)**2 for r in IRREPS8) for k in csize}\n    amb = [k for k in sorted(csize) if absq[k] > 0.5 and k != 0]\n    hits = []\n    for bits in range(2 ** len(amb)):\n        row = {k: float(np.sqrt(max(absq[k], 0.0))) for k in csize}\n        for j, k in enumerate(amb):\n            if (bits >> j) & 1:\n                row[k] = -row[k]\n        vec = np.array([row[k] for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES])\n        if (all(abs(np.mean(vec * others_vec[r])) < 1e-9 for r in IRREPS8)\n                and abs(np.mean(vec * vec) - 1.0) < 1e-9):\n            hits.append(vec)\n    assert len(hits) == 1, f\"R5 sign solution not unique: {len(hits)}\"\n    tab = {r: others_vec[r] for r in IRREPS8}\n    tab[\"R5\"] = hits[0]\n    return tab\n\nCHARTAB = _build_chartab()\n\n_orth = np.array([[np.mean(CHARTAB[a] * CHARTAB[b]) for b in IRREP_NAMES]\n                  for a in IRREP_NAMES])\nassert np.abs(_orth - np.eye(9)).max() < 1e-9\n\nDIMS = {name: int(round(CHARTAB[name][0])) for name in IRREP_NAMES}\nD_RHO = {\"R0\": 0, \"R1\": 1, \"R3\": 2, \"R6\": 3, \"R7\": 4, \"R8\": 5, \"R4\": 6, \"R5\": 6, \"R2\": 7}\n\ndef multiplicity(rho, n):\n    return int(round(np.mean(np.array([chiV(n, k) for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES]) * CHARTAB[rho])))\n\n# ---- unitarization of symmetric powers ----\ndef _sym_gram(n):\n    \"\"\"Diagonal Gram for Sym^n in monomial basis: G_kk = k!(n-k)!/n!\"\"\"\n    fn = factorial(n)\n    return np.array([factorial(k) * factorial(n - k) / fn for k in range(n + 1)])\n\ndef pi_n_mono(n, q):\n    if n == 0:\n        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n    return sym_power(quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float)), n)\n\ndef _pi_n_cg_recurrence(n, U):\n    \"\"\"Sym^n in unitarized (weight) basis via CG recurrence. Stable at all n.\"\"\"\n    D = U.copy()\n    for step in range(1, n):\n        dim_old = step + 1\n        dim_new = step + 2\n        D_pad = np.zeros((dim_new, dim_new), dtype=complex)\n        D_pad[:dim_old, :dim_old] = D\n        D_pm = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n        D_pm[:, 1:] = D_pad[:, :dim_new - 1]\n        D_mp = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n        D_mp[1:, :] = D_pad[:dim_new - 1, :]\n        D_mm = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n        D_mm[1:, 1:] = D_pad[:dim_new - 1, :dim_new - 1]\n        i_arr = np.arange(dim_new, dtype=float)\n        cg_op = np.sqrt(np.clip((dim_old - i_arr) / dim_old, 0, None))\n        cg_om = np.sqrt(np.clip(i_arr / dim_old, 0, None))\n        CG_pp = cg_op[:, None] * cg_op[None, :]\n        CG_pm = cg_op[:, None] * cg_om[None, :]\n        CG_mp = cg_om[:, None] * cg_op[None, :]\n        CG_mm = cg_om[:, None] * cg_om[None, :]\n        D = (CG_pp * D_pad * U[0, 0] + CG_pm * D_pm * U[0, 1] +\n             CG_mp * D_mp * U[1, 0] + CG_mm * D_mm * U[1, 1])\n    return D\n\ndef pi_n_unitary(n, q):\n    if n == 0:\n        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n    U = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n    if n <= 40:\n        g = _sym_gram(n)\n        Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n        Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n        return Dh @ sym_power(U, n) @ Dih\n    return _pi_n_cg_recurrence(n, U)\n\n# ---- extract R2 from V_7 via Reynolds projector ----\ndef _build_R2_matrices():\n    \"\"\"Extract R2 (dim 2) from V_7|_{2I} using the character-weighted Reynolds projector.\"\"\"\n    d = 8  # dim V_7\n    all_V7 = np.zeros((120, d, d), dtype=complex)\n    for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n        all_V7[i] = pi_n_unitary(7, q)\n    chi_R2 = CHARTAB[\"R2\"]\n    P = np.zeros((d, d), dtype=complex)\n    for i in range(120):\n        P += chi_R2[i].conj() * all_V7[i]\n    P *= 2.0 / 120.0\n    eigvals, eigvecs = np.linalg.eigh(P)\n    mask = eigvals > 0.5\n    assert mask.sum() == 2, f\"expected rank 2, got {mask.sum()}\"\n    U = eigvecs[:, mask]  # 8x2\n    R2_mats = np.zeros((120, 2, 2), dtype=complex)\n    for i in range(120):\n        R2_mats[i] = U.conj().T @ all_V7[i] @ U\n    return R2_mats\n\n_R2_ALL = _build_R2_matrices()\n\n# ---- build R4 = Sym^2(R2) ----\ndef _sym2_of_mat(M):\n    \"\"\"Sym^2 of a 2x2 matrix in the monomial basis {u^2, uv, v^2}.\"\"\"\n    return sym_power(M, 2)\n\ndef _build_R4_matrices():\n    \"\"\"R4 = unitarized Sym^2(R2).\n    R2 is already unitary (det 1), so Sym^2(R2) uses the same Gram as Sym^2(SU2).\"\"\"\n    g = _sym_gram(2)\n    Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n    Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n    R4_mats = np.zeros((120, 3, 3), dtype=complex)\n    for i in range(120):\n        R4_mats[i] = Dh @ _sym2_of_mat(_R2_ALL[i]) @ Dih\n    return R4_mats\n\n_R4_ALL = _build_R4_matrices()\n\n# ---- build R5 = R1 tensor R2 ----\ndef _build_R5_matrices():\n    R5_mats = np.zeros((120, 4, 4), dtype=complex)\n    for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n        R1 = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n        R5_mats[i] = np.kron(R1, _R2_ALL[i])\n    return R5_mats\n\n_R5_ALL = _build_R5_matrices()\n\n# ---- precompute all rep matrices ----\ndef _precompute_all():\n    \"\"\"Precompute all 120 matrices for every sector.\"\"\"\n    all_reps = {}\n    for rho in IRREP_NAMES:\n        d = DIMS[rho]\n        mats = np.zeros((120, d, d), dtype=complex)\n        for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n            mats[i] = _rep_rho_single(rho, i)\n        all_reps[rho] = mats\n    return all_reps\n\ndef _rep_rho_single(rho, idx):\n    q = G120[idx]\n    if rho == \"R0\":\n        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n    elif rho == \"R1\":\n        return quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n    elif rho == \"R2\":\n        return _R2_ALL[idx]\n    elif rho == \"R3\":\n        return pi_n_unitary(2, q)\n    elif rho == \"R4\":\n        return _R4_ALL[idx]\n    elif rho == \"R5\":\n        return _R5_ALL[idx]\n    elif rho == \"R6\":\n        return pi_n_unitary(3, q)\n    elif rho == \"R7\":\n        return pi_n_unitary(4, q)\n    elif rho == \"R8\":\n        return pi_n_unitary(5, q)\n    raise ValueError(f\"unknown sector: {rho}\")\n\nALL_REPS = _precompute_all()\n\ndef rep_rho(rho, idx):\n    \"\"\"Unitary representation matrix of G120[idx] in sector rho.\"\"\"\n    return ALL_REPS[rho][idx]\n\ndef rep_rho_quat(rho, q):\n    \"\"\"Unitary representation matrix of quaternion q in sector rho.\n    q must be in G120 (found by nearest-neighbor lookup).\"\"\"\n    dists = np.sqrt(((G120 - q)**2).sum(axis=1))\n    idx = int(np.argmin(dists))\n    assert dists[idx] < 1e-9, f\"quaternion not in 2I\"\n    return ALL_REPS[rho][idx]\n\n# ---- verification ----\ndef _verify_reps():\n    for rho in IRREP_NAMES:\n        d = DIMS[rho]\n        mats = ALL_REPS[rho]\n        # unitarity\n        for i in range(120):\n            M = mats[i]\n            assert M.shape == (d, d)\n            assert np.abs(M @ M.conj().T - np.eye(d)).max() < 1e-9, \\\n                f\"{rho}[{i}]: not unitary\"\n        # character\n        for i in range(120):\n            tr = np.trace(mats[i]).real\n            expected = CHARTAB[rho][i]\n            assert abs(tr - expected) < 1e-7, \\\n                f\"{rho}[{i}]: trace {tr} != {expected}\"\n        # homomorphism on full group\n        for _ in range(50):\n            a, b = np.random.randint(0, 120, 2)\n            p = qmul(G120[a], G120[b])\n            pidx = int(np.argmin(np.sqrt(((G120 - p)**2).sum(axis=1))))\n            err = np.abs(mats[a] @ mats[b] - mats[pidx]).max()\n            assert err < 1e-9, f\"{rho}: homomorphism error {err}\"\n        # rho(-1) = +-I\n        minus1_idx = int(np.argmin(np.sqrt(((G120 - np.array([-1, 0, 0, 0]))**2).sum(axis=1))))\n        Mm = mats[minus1_idx]\n        assert np.abs(np.abs(Mm) - np.eye(d)).max() < 1e-9, f\"{rho}: rho(-1) not +-I\"\n\n_verify_reps()\n","numLines":372,"startLine":1,"totalLines":372}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4742938d-57f0-48db-9d40-e56f4fbcc1e8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
